; `bs-cost-dust.journal` plus the one thing a REAL journal has that the tidy
; fixture did not: dollars written finer than a cent.
;
; This is the fixture behind the one-hundredth floor under
; `BalanceSheetReport::balanced`, and it is a regression test for a shipped bug.
;
; What went wrong
; ---------------
; The first tolerance was "one unit of the commodity's own WRITTEN precision",
; read off the widest `Dec.places` the journal writes for that commodity. On
; `bs-cost-dust.journal` every dollar posting is written to the cent, so the
; threshold came out at `$0.01` and the `$0.00227970` of cost dust was correctly
; called dust.
;
; Patrick's real journal also writes dollars at three and four places —
; brokerage interest, FX conversions and dividend reinvestments all do — so
; `p($)` was 4 there, the threshold collapsed to `$0.0001`, and the SAME
; `$0.00227970` was called an imbalance. The warning fired on a journal hledger
; accepts. The precision rule made the tolerance a function of the most finely
; written posting anywhere in the book, which has nothing to do with how large a
; cost-multiplication residue can get.
;
; The third transaction below is that one line, reduced: `$0.0327` of monthly
; brokerage interest. It balances exactly and so moves the residual not at all —
; it only moves the PRECISION. Under the old rule this file read
; `balanced == false`; under the one-hundredth floor it reads `balanced == true`,
; exactly like the fixture it was built from.
;
; Both tools still call the file valid
; ------------------------------------
;   $ hledger -f fixtures/reports/bs-cent-floor.journal check
;   (silent, exit 0)
;
; hledger's transaction-balance test tolerates half a unit at the precision each
; ENTRY was written at, and that is per transaction: the fractional lot is
; written to the cent, so it is judged against half a cent whatever the interest
; entry says. hledger carries the same residue and hides it behind display
; rounding (hledger 1.52):
;
;   $ hledger -f fixtures/reports/bs-cent-floor.journal bal -B -c '$1000.00000000'
;         $2388.03270000  assets
;       $-10000.03270000  equity, income
;       ----------------
;            $0.00227970

account assets:bank:checking   ; type: C
account assets:broker:cash     ; type: C
account assets:broker:vti      ; type: A
account equity:opening         ; type: E
account income:interest        ; type: R

2026-01-01 opening balance
    assets:bank:checking        $10,000.00
    equity:opening             $-10,000.00

2026-02-01 buy a fractional lot
    assets:broker:vti            26.2690 VTI @ $289.7713
    assets:bank:checking      $-7,612.00

2026-03-01 monthly brokerage interest
    assets:broker:cash              $0.0327
    income:interest                $-0.0327
